 derivative of $y =  3\\cos x  + 4\\sin x + 2x^3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "31b9e2d8-1458-50b3-a920-703476825420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059503 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3cos⁡x+4sin⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059504 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059504 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the slope of $y = 4\\tan x + 5x$ at $x = 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae922c00-2ff5-5a46-805a-62955bc5a08a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059504 #edb/ref 1074 "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4tan⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{sec}^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059675 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059675 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186287884 #ed