 [17592186059676 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483528 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059676 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483538 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059676 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059676 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186483539 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059676 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059676 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1045 "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186060528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186059951 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9be3f59b-09db-52f4-97b2-cfb6afd98870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060528 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060528 #edb/ref 1061 12320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060528 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "970c03a9-20ec-54a6-81c1-0b04852bff2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060528 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060529 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060529 #edb/ref 1061 12321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060529 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "727b4e4a-8b24-5865-8b3f-cc8d94213ab1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060529 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060530 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060530 #edb/ref 1061 12322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ba7adb6-6e48-5f3e-af46-a386f761686f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060530 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060531 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060531 #edb/ref 1061 12324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060531 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58bef47e-2b3d-546b-87f7-1097147174c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060531 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060532 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060532 #edb/ref 1061 12325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060532 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb4d0827-6392-5186-8463-5ccc6e4e3392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060532 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060533 #edb/ref 1061 12326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060533 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b5a3334-fa23-5bc8-bf82-7d80f190e65d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060533 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060893 #edb/ref 1064 "The derivatives of sine, cosine, and tangent are shown in the table below:\n\n| $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin x$ | $\\cos x$ |\n| $\\cos x$ | $-\\sin x$ |\n| $\\tan x$ | $\\sec^2 x$ |\n\nNow that we know the derivatives of the standard trigonometric functions, we can differentiate any combination of them by using the properties of differentiation.\n\nFor example, let's find the derivative of $y = \\cos x + \\sin x.$\n\nHere, we can use the fact that the derivative of the sum is equal to the sum of the derivatives, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll derive the derivatives shown in the table at the end of this lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060893 #edb/ref 1065 2618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060893 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dca4e1b4-5f89-5c88-9f24-b4671410d644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060893 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060894 #edb/ref 1064 "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)+\\operatorname{sin}(h)\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060894 #edb/ref 1065 2619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060894 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8a25dc2a-846b-5554-9cc7-682b81f17e0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060894 #edb/ref 1067 "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Sine" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060895 #edb/ref 1064 "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)\\operatorname{cos}(h)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)-\\operatorname{cos}(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(x)(\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(h)-1}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060895 #edb/ref 1065 2620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060895 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40bcba62-6d88-5cfd-9d89-e773514e5a63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060895 #edb/ref 1067 "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Cosine" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060896 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also prove that $(\\tan x)'= \\sec^2 x$ using limits, though it's a bit cumbersome. \n\nA faster way is to use a rule of differentiation known as the **quotient rule**. \n\nYou'll learn about the quotient rule very soon!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060896 #edb/ref 1065 2621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060896 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "32a1aa14-0e0a-53c1-a40c-e728e4b231c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060896 #edb/ref 1067 "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483527 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483532 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483527 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483527 #edb/ref 1071 "The derivative of $y=2x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483527 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06ead9d9-ba18-5b78-9c0b-6c3f9d9d335d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483527 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =2-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483536 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483528 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+2tan⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "16f98c04-c89a-5e4f-bf27-ed015bececed" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483528 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+2tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})+2({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186483537 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483538 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+3tan⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec85ab69-3759-5a26-ad41-7f26f7d7092f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483538 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+3tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})+3({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{\\sqrt{x}}+3{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483539 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186059676.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483539 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "0b99f9c0-8919-547c-a6d7-86a2865bf32a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489467 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=24 x - 60 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "308d927b-9585-594c-b5e7-da139c2cdf56" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489467 #edb/ref 1074 "The slope is the derivative evaluated at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24 x - 60 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &= (60 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(24) \\\\ &= 60 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=24+60\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=24+60\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $54.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489494 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489470 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489470 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=22 x - 55 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489470 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44191e59-3ef1-56f2-b3ae-15156e95a80d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489470 #edb/ref 1074 "The slope is the derivative evaluated at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(22 x - 55 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &= (55 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(22) \\\\ &= 55 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=22+55\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=22+55\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{99}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $\\frac{99}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489478 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489482 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489478 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489478 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the derivative of $y=- 16 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{8}{x^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489478 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7af3519e-f7d3-5ca4-a6bd-1048972ce066" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489478 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- 16 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{8}{x^{2}}) \\\\ &= (16 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(- \\frac{16}{x^{3}}) \\\\ &= 16 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{16}{x^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489491 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489489 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489491 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489491 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=16 x - 40 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489491 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddd31c3c-768d-5290-8e36-3f5b830ff739" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489491 #edb/ref 1074 "The slope is the derivative evaluated at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16 x - 40 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &= (40 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(16) \\\\ &= 40 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=16+40\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=16+40\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $36.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489493 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489484 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489493 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489493 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=6 x - 15 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e697136f-ba37-5c15-b682-60c32dbf1e52" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489493 #edb/ref 1074 "The slope is the derivative evaluated at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6 x - 15 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &= (15 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(6) \\\\ &= 15 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=6+15\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=6+15\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{27}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $\\frac{27}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489497 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186059675.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186489497 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "2db58723-b530-57aa-afb2-4f64d6a53f46" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
